---
description: Each backend service must expose and register its own health check
globs: backend/services/**/*.py
alwaysApply: true
---

# Service health checks

`/health` is an **aggregator**, not a generic API ping. Each service under `backend/services/{name}/` owns its health.

When adding a service (auth, food, nutrition, …):

1. Add `{name}_health.py` in that service (example: `services/auth/services/auth_health.py`).
2. Check that service’s real dependencies (auth → Neon). Reuse `infrastructure.database.health.check_database` for DB; do not duplicate connection logic.
3. Return a dict with at least `"status": "healthy" | "unhealthy"`. Never include `DATABASE_URL` or secrets.
4. **API / data services** (auth, users): register the check in `core/health.py` `SERVICE_CHECKS`. `GET /health` includes them and returns **503** if any registered check is unhealthy.
5. **ML inference services** (food): expose `GET /{name}/health` as **readiness** (model loaded vs unavailable). Do **not** register food on the `/health` aggregator so a missing ONNX file cannot take down auth/users. `POST /predict` still returns 503 when the model is not ready.
6. Startup logs one line per service: `[✓] AUTH       HEALTHY` or `[✗] AUTH       UNHEALTHY`. Food may log `MODEL UNAVAILABLE` without failing process liveness.

`GET /health` must include every **registered** (non-ML) service and return **503** if any of those is unhealthy.

```text
services/food/services/food_health.py     ✅
GET /api/v1/food/health                     ✅  (readiness; 503 if model down)
core/health.py SERVICE_CHECKS["food"]     ❌  (would 503 the whole API)

Only a root /health with hardcoded "api"/"database" keys for new work  ❌
```
